  urldate = {2022-03-10},
  abstract = {We provide a general framework for using Monte Carlo methods in dynamic\textbackslash nsystems and discuss its wide applications. Under this framework,\textbackslash nseveral currently available techniques are studied and generalized\textbackslash nto accommodate more complex features. All of these methods are partial\textbackslash ncombinations of three ingredients: importance sampling and resampling,\textbackslash nrejection sampling, and Markov chain iterations. We provide guidelines\textbackslash non how they should be used and under what circumstance each method\textbackslash nis most suitable. Through the analysis of differences and connections,\textbackslash nwe consolidate these methods into a generic algorithm by combining\textbackslash ndesirable features. In addition, we propose a general use of Rao-Blackwellization\textbackslash nto improve performance. Examples from econometrics and engineering\textbackslash nare presented to demonstrate the importance of Rao-Blackwellization\textbackslash nand to compare different Monte Carlo procedures.}
}

